00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
we're going to begin this hour with  startling new coronavirus numbers and a  reported wrinkle in pfizer's smoothly  laid out vaccine plans  the u.s surpassed 14 million confirmed  cases yesterday  a day when there were more than 217 000  new cases and more than 276  000 people fellow americans alive on  january 1st have since  died of the virus with the higher  highest daily  death toll yesterday than we've had ever  before  that's not good according to the wall  street journal pfizer ran into a supply  issue last month and had to cut in half  the number of doses that it planned to  distribute the company still expects to  roll out more than a billion doses  worldwide in 2021  vaccinations start in the uk this month  in a cbs news interview  with major garrett yesterday dr anthony  fauci criticized that country's plans  while also praising the fda  they really rushed through that approval  the fda the united states of america  food and drug administration  is the gold standard of regulation  they're doing it in a very careful way  appropriately in the meantime  president-elect joe biden now says he  plans to ask all of us to wear a mask  for his first 100 days  in office he also wants to require mask  wearing in certain places like  federal buildings chief washington  correspondent major garrett joins us to  discuss his interview with dr  fauci and this week's political  headlines major good morning good  morning  dr apache has since walked back his  comments to you  about the uk rushing to approve a  vaccine he told the bbc  uh he has great faith in health  officials there  but he clearly was concerned about it he  was and you can't walk back  as definitive a statement as dr fauci  made to me which is  their process is different than ours  ours is more rigorous  in dr fauci's words not mine he said the  brits essentially looked at the pfizer  data from a press release  and approved the vaccine the food and  drug administration does not do that  it goes to the underlying data  scrutinizes everything before it gives  emergency use authorization and then i  also asked him i said well the head of  the fda has been brought to the white  house twice this week by the chief of  staff mark meadows  do you consider that to be overt  political pressure dr fauci said no  it's about explaining the fda process  and getting white house backing of that  i said would it help in that context for  the white house to put out a statement  saying exactly that dr fauci said it  would  i guess we'll all wait for that major  president-elect biden has already  made a point of of asking dr fauci to  stay on  as his chief medical advisor how  significant do you think that is  it's important and the most important  thing that happened yesterday revealed  also on my show  is that yesterday for the first time via  zoom  the transition landing team for  president-elect biden met with  dr fauci and all of his team i said how  often do you expect those conversations  to go on he said  almost on a daily basis from now on in i  said would you prefer that those  conversations have occurred  and started sooner he said of course  every transition that begins earlier is  a better one but we are where we are  let's talk about the president's  baseless claims so far about  widespread voter fraud even attorney  general bill barr  said well that's not exactly the case is  his job safe it's not exactly reassuring  when the  when you're asked about the president  says well ask me in a few  weeks well a few weeks is inauguration  day number one  number two the senate and the house are  trying to race through  three enormous legislative priorities in  about a week and a half  is there going to be a stimulus bill to  deal with covid19 are we going to keep  the government open and operating are we  going to  pass the national defense authorization  act all three are very important and  you're seeing republicans move away from  the president's position  on all three of those that suggests  they're already thinking about how to  deal with the post-trump reality  even though the president is not and on  that basis  also lindsey graham a staunch defender  of the president said yesterday  where's the evidence well when bill barr  and lindsey graham are walking away from  you president trump you know you have  very few allies left in washington  and you better start thinking  differently about what you're asserting  and trying to allege  yeah do you think bill barr's job is  safe major  it probably not as not as safe as it was  a week ago but what's the what's the  point  you're going to sack your attorney  general and then what the senate is  going to confirm a new one no  you're just leaving the attorney  generalship vacant and  the department of justice rudderless and  leadership-less  is that really the way you want to go  out mr president i mean it could happen  i'm not ruling it out  but what's the point there really isn't  a governing point whatsoever  other than the president expressing his  rage as someone who won't do precisely  what he wants him to do  even though the job of the attorney  general is to apply the law  as it's written not as the president  wants it to be interpreted major  meanwhile the president's headed to  georgia tomorrow as he continues  his claims of voter fraud there are a  lot of concerns among republicans  that he may be suppressing the party's  own vote there  right he'll run up the vote in the rural  areas where the trump supporters believe  he's onto something but he could very  likely suppress it in the suburban areas  where the republicans don't buy this  assertion made over and over without any  backing that there is something wrong in  the way georgia conducted its election  and that's a tension that is playing  itself out there it's got republicans  attention here they want the president  to stop and give a unified message  look elect these two republicans back  their agenda i'm with them  and set aside his grievances about  george's  election conduct they will all wait to  see  what he does what's the constant here  the president's at the center of the  spotlight  where he always wants to be major  garrett thank you major

